/* Coach content: repertoire presets, annotated master games, the 26-week plan. Generated by tools/build_app_data.py */
const COACH_REP=[{"name":"English Opening (White)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: English Opening (White)\"]\n\n1. c4 e5 (1... c5 {Symmetrical.} 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. g3 g6 4. Bg2 Bg7 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. O-O O-O 7. d4 cxd4 8. Nxd4 {Open centre. White's g2 bishop is strong.}) (1... Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 (2... g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O {A flexible fianchetto: d3, Rb1 and b4.}) (2... d5 3. cxd5 Nxd5 4. g3 g6 5. Bg2 Nb6 6. Nf3 Bg7 7. O-O {Grünfeld-style, with an extra tempo.}) 3. Nf3 d5 4. d4 Be7 5. Bf4 {Queen's Gambit Declined with Bf4.}) (1... e6 2. Nc3 d5 3. d4 {Transposes to the Queen's Gambit.}) (1... c6 2. Nf3 d5 3. e3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. b3 {Slow and flexible: Bb2, Qc2, then d4.}) 2. Nc3 Nf6 (2... Nc6 3. g3 g6 4. Bg2 Bg7 5. d3 d6 6. e4 {Botvinnik System: a grip on d5.} 6... Nge7 7. Nge2 O-O 8. O-O {Plan: f4 or Be3, Qd2, then f4.}) 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. g3 d5 (4... Bb4 5. Bg2 O-O 6. O-O e4 7. Ng5 Bxc3 8. bxc3 Re8 9. f3 exf3 10. Nxf3 d5 11. d4 {A strong centre.}) 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Bg2 Nb6 7. O-O Be7 8. d3 O-O 9. a3 Be6 10. b4 {A Reversed Sicilian: Black's Dragon set-up a tempo down. White attacks on the queenside.} *\n"},{"name":"Italian Game (White)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Italian Game (White)\"]\n\n1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 (2... d6 {Philidor. Take the centre and develop.} 3. d4 Nf6 (3... exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Bf4 O-O 7. Qd2 {Castle long and push g4 if Black is passive.}) 4. Nc3 Nbd7 5. Bc4 Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 c6 8. a4 {Stop ...b5. White has more space.}) (2... Nf6 {Petrov. Never play 3...Nxe4? 4.Qe2!} 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. Nc3 Nxc3 6. dxc3 Be7 7. Be3 O-O 8. Qd2 {Castle queenside, then push the kingside pawns.}) 3. Bc4 Bc5 (3... Nf6 {Two Knights. We play the quiet d3, so the Fried Liver chaos never starts.} 4. d3 Be7 (4... Bc5 5. c3 {Back in the main line.}) 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 Na5 8. Bb5 a6 9. Ba4 b5 10. Bc2 c5 11. Nbd2 {Plan: Nf1-g3, h3, d4. A slow Ruy Lopez setup.}) (3... Be7 {Hungarian. Black is passive, so grab space.} 4. d4 d6 5. d5 Nb8 6. Bd3 {Then c4, Nc3, O-O: a space advantage and an easy game.}) (3... Nd4 {A trap. Do NOT play 4.Nxe5? Qg5! with a big attack.} 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. O-O {Calm. Then c3 and d4. Black has wasted time.}) 4. c3 Nf6 (4... d6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb6 7. Nc3 {A full centre for free.}) 5. d3 d6 (5... a6 6. O-O d6 7. a4 {Gives the c4 bishop a retreat on a2.}) 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 a6 8. a4 Ba7 9. h3 h6 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Bb3 Be6 12. Bc2 {The Giuoco Pianissimo plan: Nf1-g3, then d3-d4 when ready. Keep the tension. Don't exchange on e6 without a reason.} *\n"},{"name":"anti-Sicilian with 2.Nf3 and 3.Bb5 (White)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: anti-Sicilian with 2.Nf3 and 3.Bb5 (White)\"]\n\n1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 (2... d6 {Moscow Variation.} 3. Bb5+ Bd7 (3... Nd7 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nc3 cxd4 6. Qxd4 e5 7. Qd3 {Plan: O-O, Nd2-c4, a4. The d5 square is yours.}) (3... Nc6 4. O-O Bd7 5. Re1 Nf6 6. c3 {Then d4. Same plan as the Italian: c3, d4.}) 4. Bxd7+ Qxd7 5. O-O Nc6 6. c3 Nf6 7. Re1 e6 8. d4 cxd4 9. cxd4 d5 10. e5 Ne4 11. Nbd2 {Trade the e4 knight, then build the kingside: Nf1-g3, Bd2, Rc1.}) (2... e6 {With e6, go into Alapin structures.} 3. c3 d5 (3... Nf6 4. e5 Nd5 5. d4 cxd4 6. cxd4 d6 7. Bc4 Nb6 8. Bb3 {A big centre and a clear plan: Nc3, O-O, Qe2.}) 4. exd5 exd5 5. d4 Nc6 6. Bb5 Bd6 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. O-O Ne7 9. Nbd2 O-O 10. Nb3 Bb6 11. Re1 {Black has an isolated d5 pawn. Fischer's recipe: blockade d4, trade minor pieces, win d5 in the endgame.}) (2... g6 3. c3 Bg7 4. d4 cxd4 5. cxd4 d5 6. e5 {Space. Black's g7 bishop bites on granite.}) (2... a6 3. c3 d5 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. d4 Nf6 6. Be2 {Simple development: O-O, c4 or Na3-c2.}) (2... Nf6 3. e5 Nd5 4. c3 Nc6 5. d4 cxd4 6. cxd4 d6 7. Bc4 {Alapin-type centre, with the bishop on its Italian square.}) 3. Bb5 g6 (3... e6 4. Bxc6 bxc6 5. d3 {Plan: b3, Nbd2, e5. Black's doubled c-pawns are the long-term target.}) (3... d6 4. O-O Bd7 5. Re1 Nf6 6. c3 {Then d4.}) 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. d3 Bg7 6. h3 Nf6 7. Nc3 O-O 8. Be3 b6 9. Qd2 {Plan: Bh6 to trade the g7 bishop, then push on the kingside. Black's extra bishop is blunted by White's pawns on light squares.} *\n"},{"name":"Advance vs French and Caro-Kann (White)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Advance vs French and Caro-Kann (White)\"]\n\n1. e4 e6 (1... c6 {Caro-Kann: same Advance idea.} 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 c5 6. Be3 {Calm development. Castle, then c3 or c4 depending on Black.}) 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 (3... b6 4. c3 Qd7 5. Nf3 Ba6 6. Bxa6 Nxa6 7. O-O {Development lead.}) 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 (5... Bd7 6. Be2 Nge7 7. Na3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5 9. Nc2 {Protects d4 forever.}) 6. a3 c4 (6... Bd7 7. b4 cxd4 8. cxd4 Rc8 9. Bb2 {Space on the queenside, solid centre.}) (6... a5 7. Bd3 Bd7 8. Bc2 {Then O-O, Re1. The e5 pawn cramps Black.}) 7. Nbd2 Na5 8. Rb1 {Prepares b4. Then play on the kingside: Be2, O-O, Re1, Nf1-g3, h4. The e5 pawn is your trump. Never give it up cheaply.} *\n"},{"name":"against rare defences to 1.e4 (White)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: against rare defences to 1.e4 (White)\"]\n\n1. e4 d5 (1... d6 {Pirc.} 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. Be3 Bg7 (4... c6 5. Qd2 b5 6. f3 {Same plan.}) 5. Qd2 c6 6. f3 b5 7. Nge2 Nbd7 8. Bh6 Bxh6 9. Qxh6 {The 150 Attack: trade the g7 bishop, castle long, push h4-h5.}) (1... g6 {Modern.} 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Same as against the Pirc: Qd2, f3, Bh6.}) (1... Nf6 {Alekhine.} 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. Nf3 Bg4 (4... dxe5 5. Nxe5 {Central knight, easy development.}) 5. Be2 e6 6. O-O Be7 7. c4 Nb6 8. Nc3 O-O 9. Be3 {Space. White's pawns cramp Black.}) (1... b6 {Owen.} 2. d4 Bb7 3. Bd3 e6 4. Nf3 c5 5. c3 Nf6 6. Qe2 {Big centre, solid.}) (1... Nc6 {Nimzowitsch.} 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 Bg4 5. Be2 e6 6. O-O {Classical development.}) 2. exd5 Qxd5 (2... Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. Nf3 g6 5. c4 Nb6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. h3 {Space and an easy game.}) 3. Nc3 Qa5 (3... Qd6 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 a6 6. g3 {Bg2 and O-O. Black's queen is a target.}) 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 c6 6. Bc4 Bf5 7. Bd2 e6 8. Qe2 {Then O-O-O or Nd5 tricks. You score 100% here already.} *\n"},{"name":"Evans Gambit, Fischer's weapon (White)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Evans Gambit, Fischer's weapon (White)\"]\n\n1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 Bxb4 (4... Bb6 {Declined.} 5. a4 a6 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Nd5 {Pressure on b6 and f6.}) 5. c3 Ba5 (5... Be7 6. d4 Na5 7. Nxe5 {White has the centre and good play.}) (5... Bc5 6. d4 exd4 7. O-O d6 8. cxd4 Bb6 {A big centre for the pawn.}) 6. d4 exd4 (6... d6 7. Qb3 Qd7 8. dxe5 Bb6 {Lasker's defence: Black gives the pawn back.}) 7. O-O dxc3 {Greedy. This is Fischer-Fine 1963.} 8. Qb3 Qe7 9. Nxc3 Nf6 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. exd5 Ne5 12. Nxe5 Qxe5 13. Bb2 Qg5 14. h4 Qxh4 15. Bxg7 Rg8 16. Rfe1+ Kd8 17. Qg3 {Black resigned. Two pawns down, but every white piece is attacking.} *\n"},{"name":"Ruy Lopez, the Spanish Game (White)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Ruy Lopez, the Spanish Game (White)\"]\n\n1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {The bishop pressures the knight that defends e5. The main fight is for the centre after c3 and d4. Other second moves for Black: see your Italian file.} 3... a6 (3... Nf6 {Berlin. Avoid the drawish Berlin endgame with the quiet 4.d3.} 4. d3 Bc5 (4... d6 5. c3 g6 6. O-O Bg7 7. Re1 O-O 8. Nbd2 {Slow build-up, then d4.}) 5. c3 O-O 6. O-O d6 7. h3 a6 8. Ba4 Ba7 9. Re1 Ne7 10. Nbd2 Ng6 11. Nf1 {The same plans as your Italian: Ng3, Be3, d4.}) (3... f5 {Schliemann.} 4. d3 fxe4 5. dxe4 Nf6 6. O-O d6 7. Nc3 {Calm development. Black's early ...f5 weakened his king and the e6 square.}) (3... Bc5 {Classical.} 4. O-O Nf6 5. c3 O-O 6. d4 Bb6 7. Bg5 {Pin the knight, then Re1 and Nbd2.}) (3... d6 {Old Steinitz.} 4. d4 Bd7 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. O-O Be7 7. Re1 exd4 8. Nxd4 O-O 9. Bf1 {More space for White.}) (3... Nd4 {Bird.} 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. O-O c6 6. Bc4 Nf6 7. Re1 {e5 is coming.}) (3... Nge7 {Cozio.} 4. c3 d6 5. d4 Bd7 6. O-O Ng6 7. Re1 {A strong centre.}) (3... g6 {Smyslov.} 4. c3 a6 5. Ba4 d6 6. d4 Bd7 7. O-O {Central pressure.}) 4. Ba4 Nf6 (4... d6 {Steinitz Deferred.} 5. c3 Bd7 6. d4 Nge7 7. Bb3 h6 8. Nbd2 Ng6 9. Nc4 {Pressure on e5.}) 5. O-O Be7 (5... Nxe4 {Open Ruy Lopez.} 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 9. c3 Bc5 10. Nbd2 O-O 11. Bc2 {Pressure on the e4 knight. White's e5 pawn gives space.}) 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 (7... O-O {Marshall Attack territory. Avoid it.} 8. a4 {Anti-Marshall.} 8... b4 (8... Bb7 9. d3 d6 10. Nbd2 {Solid.}) 9. d3 d6 10. a5 Be6 11. Nbd2 {Easy play, no Marshall gambit.}) 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Na5 (9... Bb7 {Zaitsev.} 10. d4 Re8 11. Nbd2 Bf8 12. a4 {Pressure on b5.}) (9... Nb8 {Breyer.} 10. d4 Nbd7 11. Nbd2 Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 13. Nf1 Bf8 14. Ng3 {The classic manoeuvre Nbd2-f1-g3.}) 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 12. Nbd2 Nc6 (12... cxd4 13. cxd4 Nc6 14. Nb3 a5 15. Be3 a4 16. Nbd2 {Chigorin main line.}) 13. d5 Nd8 14. a4 {Karpov-Unzicker 1974 (in Knight'nMare's Guess the move): close the centre, then squeeze on both wings with a4, b4 and later f4.} *\n"},{"name":"Scotch Game (White)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Scotch Game (White)\"]\n\n1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 {Kasparov's favourite. Open the centre at once, before Black is ready.} 3... exd4 (3... d6 4. Bb5 Bd7 5. Nc3 exd4 6. Nxd4 {More space and easy development.}) (3... Nxd4 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. Qxd4 {A central queen and a development lead.}) 4. Nxd4 Nf6 (4... Bc5 5. Nxc6 Qf6 (5... bxc6 6. Bd3 d6 7. O-O Nf6 8. Nd2 O-O 9. Nb3 Bb6 10. Bf4 {Pressure on d6. Then Qf3 and Rae1. Black's pawns are split.}) 6. Qd2 dxc6 7. Nc3 Be6 8. Na4 Rd8 9. Bd3 Bd4 10. O-O {Kasparov's line. White hunts the bishop pair and has a healthier structure.}) (4... Qh4 {Steinitz. Greedy.} 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. Be2 Qxe4 7. Nb5 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Kd8 9. O-O {A pawn down, but a huge development lead and Black's king is stuck.}) (4... d6 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Bc4 Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. h3 {More space.}) (4... g6 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. e5 Ng8 9. Bd4 {Trade the g7 bishop.}) 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 7. Qe2 Nd5 8. c4 Ba6 (8... Nb6 9. Nd2 Qe6 10. b3 a5 11. Bb2 Bb4 12. a3 Bxd2+ 13. Qxd2 d5 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Rc1 {Pressure on c7 and the c-file.}) 9. b3 g6 (9... O-O-O 10. g3 Re8 11. Bb2 f6 12. Bg2 {Strong pressure on the long diagonal.}) 10. f4 f6 11. Ba3 {The Mieses Variation. The e5 pawn cramps Black, and the a3 bishop pins the queen.} *\n"},{"name":"Queen's Gambit (White, against 1...d5)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Queen's Gambit (White, against 1...d5)\"]\n\n1. d4 d5 {Against 1...Nf6, 1...f5 and others: see the London System file.} 2. c4 e6 (2... dxc4 {QGA.} 3. e4 e5 (3... Nf6 4. e5 Nd5 5. Bxc4 Nb6 6. Bb3 Nc6 7. Ne2 Bf5 8. Nbc3 e6 9. O-O {Space and development.}) (3... Nc6 4. Be3 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. d5 Ne7 7. Bxc4 {A big centre.}) 4. Nf3 exd4 5. Bxc4 Bb4+ 6. Nbd2 Nc6 7. O-O Nf6 8. e5 Nd5 9. Nb3 {White wins the d4 pawn back with active pieces.}) (2... c6 {Slav.} 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 (4... e6 {Semi-Slav.} 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Qc2 Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O dxc4 9. Bxc4 b5 10. Bd3 Bb7 11. e4 {The centre rolls.}) 5. a4 Bf5 6. e3 e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 8. O-O Nbd7 9. Qe2 O-O 10. e4 Bg6 11. Bd3 {White gets e4 in with a slight edge.}) (2... Nc6 {Chigorin.} 3. Nf3 Bg4 4. cxd5 Bxf3 5. gxf3 Qxd5 6. e3 e5 7. Nc3 Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 9. bxc3 {The two bishops and a strong centre.}) (2... e5 {Albin Countergambit. Trap: never 4.e3? Bb4+ 5.Bd2 dxe3!, and 6.Bxb4?? exf2+ 7.Ke2 fxg1=N+! is the Lasker Trap.} 3. dxe5 d4 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. g3 Be6 (5... Bg4 6. Bg2 Qd7 7. O-O O-O-O 8. Qb3 {White's attack on the queenside comes first.}) 6. Nbd2 Qd7 7. Bg2 O-O-O 8. O-O {Win back d4 later. White's king is safer.}) (2... Bf5 {Baltic.} 3. cxd5 Bxb1 4. Qa4+ c6 5. dxc6 Nxc6 6. Rxb1 {An extra pawn.}) 3. Nc3 Nf6 (3... Be7 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 c6 6. e3 Bf5 7. Nge2 Nd7 8. Ng3 Bg6 9. Bd3 {Trade the light bishops, castle, then Rc1 and the minority attack.}) (3... c5 {Tarrasch.} 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. g3 Nf6 7. Bg2 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Bg5 cxd4 10. Nxd4 h6 11. Be3 {Target the isolated d5 pawn.}) 4. cxd5 {The Exchange Variation: a clear plan for life.} 4... exd5 5. Bg5 Be7 (5... c6 6. e3 Bf5 7. Qf3 Bg6 8. Bxf6 Qxf6 9. Qxf6 gxf6 {Black's pawns are damaged.}) 6. e3 O-O (6... c6 7. Qc2 Nbd7 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 11. f3 {Kasparov's plan: e3-e4 in the centre.}) 7. Bd3 Nbd7 8. Nf3 c6 9. Qc2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 11. Rab1 Ng6 12. b4 {The minority attack: b4-b5 to leave Black with a weak c6 pawn.} *\n"},{"name":"London System (White, a complete 1.d4 system)","side":"w","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: London System (White, a complete 1.d4 system)\"]\n\n1. d4 d5 (1... Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 (2... e6 3. e3 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nd2 d5 6. Ngf3 Bd6 7. Bg3 O-O 8. Bd3 {The London set-up. Plan: Ne5, f4, Qf3 and a kingside attack.}) (2... c5 3. e3 Nc6 4. c3 Qb6 5. Qb3 {Trade queens if Black wants. The structure stays solid.}) (2... d6 3. Nf3 g6 4. e3 Bg7 5. h3 O-O 6. Be2 {Calm. c4 or c3 next.}) 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Be2 d6 6. h3 c5 7. c3 b6 8. O-O Bb7 9. Nbd2 {Against the King's Indian set-up: a safe structure, with e4 or Bh2 and Qc2 later.}) (1... f5 {Dutch.} 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. e3 e6 4. Nf3 b6 5. h3 Bb7 6. Nbd2 Be7 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O d6 9. c3 {Then Qe2 and e4.}) (1... e6 2. Bf4 c5 3. e3 Nc6 4. c3 d5 5. Nd2 {Back in the main line.}) (1... c5 2. d5 Nf6 3. Nc3 d6 4. e4 g6 5. Nf3 Bg7 6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O {A Benoni with a solid extra centre.}) 2. Bf4 Nf6 (2... c5 3. e3 Nc6 4. c3 Qb6 5. Qc1 Nf6 6. Be2 Bf5 7. Nf3 e6 8. Nh4 {Guard b2, then chase the bishop. Solid.}) 3. e3 c5 (3... e6 4. Nf3 c5 5. c3 Nc6 6. Nbd2 Bd6 7. Bg3 O-O 8. Bd3 b6 9. Ne5 Bb7 10. O-O {Castle first, then f4, Qf3 and a kingside attack.}) (3... Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 c5 7. c3 Nc6 8. Nbd2 Bd6 9. Bg3 {Solid. e4 next.}) 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nd2 e6 6. Ngf3 Bd6 7. Bg3 O-O 8. Bd3 b6 9. Ne5 {The classic London structure: Ne5, f4 and Qf3 with a kingside attack.} *\n"},{"name":"Grünfeld Defence (Black)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Grünfeld Defence (Black)\"]\n\n1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 {Against 2.Nf3, 2.Bg5 and the London: see the King's Indian file.} 3. Nc3 (3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O c5 {Strike at d4.}) (3. f3 {Anti-Grünfeld.} 3... d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nb6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O f5 {Hit the centre at once.}) 3... d5 {The Grünfeld, Kasparov's weapon. Let White build a big centre, then attack it with ...c5, ...Bg7 and ...Nc6.} 4. cxd5 (4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Qb3 {Russian System.} 5... dxc4 6. Qxc4 O-O 7. e4 a6 8. Be2 b5 9. Qb3 c5 10. dxc5 Bb7 {The Hungarian Variation. Active queenside play.}) (4. Bf4 Bg7 5. e3 c5 6. dxc5 Qa5 7. Rc1 dxc4 8. Bxc4 O-O 9. Nf3 Qxc5 10. Bb3 Nc6 11. O-O Qa5 12. h3 {Equal and active.}) (4. Bg5 Ne4 5. Bf4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Bg7 7. e3 c5 8. Nf3 O-O {Pressure on c3 and d4.}) (4. g3 Bg7 5. Bg2 O-O 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Nf3 Nb6 8. O-O Nc6 9. e3 Re8 {Then ...e5.}) 4... Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Bg7 7. Nf3 (7. Bc4 c5 8. Ne2 Nc6 9. Be3 O-O 10. O-O Bg4 11. f3 Na5 12. Bd3 cxd4 13. cxd4 Be6 {The classical main line: blockade d5 and pressure d4.}) (7. Be3 c5 8. Rc1 Qa5 9. Qd2 cxd4 10. cxd4 Qxd2+ 11. Kxd2 O-O {An endgame where Black's pressure on d4 balances White's centre.}) 7... c5 8. Rb1 (8. Be3 Qa5 9. Qd2 Nc6 10. Rc1 cxd4 11. cxd4 Qxd2+ 12. Kxd2 O-O 13. d5 Rd8 14. Ke1 Na5 {Equal. In Kramnik-Kasparov 2000 (Guess the move), Black played 9...Bg4 10.Rb1 a6 and lost a pawn after 11.Rxb7.}) 8... O-O 9. Be2 cxd4 10. cxd4 Qa5+ 11. Bd2 Qxa2 12. O-O Bg4 13. Bg5 h6 14. Be3 Nc6 15. d5 Na5 {The modern main line. Black grabbed a pawn. White has central pressure and initiative for it.} *\n"},{"name":"Caro-Kann (Black)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Caro-Kann (Black)\"]\n\n1. e4 c6 2. d4 (2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 Bg4 4. h3 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 e6 6. d4 Nf6 7. Bd3 dxe4 8. Nxe4 Nxe4 9. Qxe4 Nd7 {Solid. ...Nf6, ...Bd6 or ...Be7, castle. White's bishops are not dangerous.}) (2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 e5 4. Ngf3 Bd6 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Re8 {A comfortable centre. See Tal-Smyslov 1959 for what Black must avoid: opening lines while behind in development.}) (2. c4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nc6 {Easy development and active pieces.}) 2... d5 3. Nc3 (3. e5 Bf5 {Advance: bishop out first, then ...e6 and ...c5.} 4. Nf3 (4. Nc3 e6 5. g4 Bg6 6. Nge2 c5 {Sharp! Strike the centre at once.}) (4. Bd3 Bxd3 5. Qxd3 e6 {Your bad bishop is gone. Easy game.}) (4. c3 e6 5. Be2 c5 {Same plan.}) 4... e6 5. Be2 c5 6. Be3 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Ne7 8. O-O Nbc6 {Trade on d4, then ...Ng6 and ...Be7. Pressure on e5 and d4.}) (3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 (4. c4 {Panov.} 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. Nf3 Be7 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 {You face an isolated pawn: blockade on d5, trade pieces, the endgame favours you. See Tal-Bronstein 1971 for White's attacking ideas.}) 4... Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bf4 Bg4 7. Qb3 Qd7 8. Nd2 e6 9. Ngf3 Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. O-O O-O {Plan: the minority attack with ...b5-b4.}) (3. f3 {Fantasy.} 3... e6 {Then ...c5 or ...Nf6. Take on e4 only when it helps you.}) 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 (6. Nf3 Nd7 7. Bd3 e6 8. O-O Ngf6 {Solid. Castle and play ...c5 later.}) 6... h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 13. Kb1 O-O {The classic Caro-Kann: no weaknesses. Plans: ...c5 or ...Qd5, trade pieces, and win the long game.} *\n"},{"name":"Slav and London (Black)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Slav and London (Black)\"]\n\n1. d4 d5 2. c4 (2. Bf4 c5 3. e3 Nc6 4. c3 Qb6 5. Qb3 c4 6. Qc2 Bf5 {Trade the light bishops on your terms, then play ...e6 and ...Nf6.}) (2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c5 4. e3 Nc6 5. c3 Qb6 6. Qb3 c4 7. Qc2 Bf5 {The same idea.}) 2... c6 3. Nf3 (3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. Nf3 e6 6. Nh4 Bg6 7. Nxg6 hxg6 {The open h-file is useful to you.}) 3... Nf6 4. Nc3 (4. e3 Bf5 5. Nc3 e6 6. Nh4 Bg6 7. Nxg6 hxg6 {Same structure.}) 4... dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. e3 e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 8. O-O Nbd7 9. Qe2 O-O {The main Slav. Same c6-d5 ideas as your Caro-Kann.} *\n"},{"name":"French Defence (Black, alternative to the Caro-Kann)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: French Defence (Black, alternative to the Caro-Kann)\"]\n\n1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 (3. e5 {Advance.} 3... c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 c4 {Gain queenside space, then ...Na5-b3 and ...Bd7.} 7. Nbd2 Na5 8. Rb1 Bd7 {Black attacks on the queenside. White attacks on the kingside. Know your side.}) (3. Nd2 {Tarrasch.} 3... c5 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. Ngf3 cxd4 6. Bc4 Qd6 7. O-O Nf6 8. Nb3 Nc6 9. Nbxd4 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 a6 {No isolated pawn, quick development, ...Bd7 and ...Bc5 or ...Be7, castle. Solid.}) (3. exd5 {Exchange.} 3... exd5 4. Bd3 Bd6 5. Nf3 Ne7 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 Bf5 {Symmetrical but your light bishop is free. Equal and easy.}) 3... Nf6 4. e5 (4. Bg5 dxe4 {The Burn Variation.} 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. Bxf6 Bxf6 7. Nf3 O-O {Two bishops. Then ...Nd7, ...b6 or ...c5.}) 4... Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 (7... a6 8. Qd2 b5 {Queenside expansion first.}) 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O a6 {Steinitz Variation. Black attacks with ...b5-b4 and ...Qa5. White goes for the kingside. Race!} *\n"},{"name":"against 1.c4 and 1.Nf3 (Black)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: against 1.c4 and 1.Nf3 (Black)\"]\n\n1. c4 (1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 c6 4. O-O Bg4 5. d3 Nbd7 6. Nbd2 e5 7. e4 dxe4 8. dxe4 Bc5 {Active pieces and a share of the centre.}) 1... c6 {Heads for Slav or Caro-Kann structures you already know.} 2. Nf3 (2. e4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Bb5 e6 8. O-O Be7 9. d4 O-O {White gets an isolated d-pawn: blockade d5 and trade pieces, as in the Panov.}) (2. d4 d5 {Back in your Slav.}) (2. Nc3 d5 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. d4 Nf6 {Exchange Slav. Symmetrical, solid.}) 2... d5 3. e3 (3. b3 Nf6 4. g3 Bf5 5. Bg2 e6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Bb2 h6 8. d3 Be7 9. Nbd2 O-O {Solid. Later ...a5-a4 to hit White's queenside.}) (3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 Bf5 5. O-O e6 6. b3 Nbd7 7. Bb2 h6 8. d3 Be7 {Same set-up.}) 3... Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. d4 Nbd7 6. Qc2 Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O dxc4 9. Bxc4 b5 10. Bd3 Bb7 {Meran-style Semi-Slav. Next ...a6 and ...c5.} *\n"},{"name":"1...e5 repair kit (Black, the lines that beat you)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: 1...e5 repair kit (Black, the lines that beat you)\"]\n\n1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 (2. Qh5 {Early queen attack.} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 g6 4. Qf3 Nf6 5. Ne2 Bg7 6. Nbc3 d6 7. d3 Bg4 {Develop with tempo against the queen. The queen sortie just costs White time.}) (2. Bc4 Nf6 3. d3 c6 4. Nf3 d5 5. Bb3 Bd6 {A big centre for Black.}) (2. f4 {King's Gambit.} 2... exf4 3. Nf3 d5 4. exd5 Nf6 5. Bb5+ c6 6. dxc6 Nxc6 7. d4 Bd6 {Modern Defence: give the pawn back and develop fast. Black is comfortable.}) (2. d4 exd4 3. Qxd4 (3. c3 {Danish.} 3... d5 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. cxd4 Nc6 6. Nf3 Bg4 7. Be2 Bb4+ 8. Nc3 Bxf3 9. Bxf3 Qc4 {Decline the gambit with ...d5. The queen on c4 stops White castling. Black is at least equal.}) 3... Nc6 4. Qe3 Nf6 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. Bd2 O-O 7. O-O-O Re8 {Every move develops with a threat.}) (2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. Nf3 Be7 {Vienna Gambit: hit back in the centre.}) 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 (3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 {Closed Ruy Lopez. Solid and well-tested.}) (3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 (4. Bc4 {Scotch Gambit.} 4... Nf6 5. e5 d5 6. Bb5 Ne4 7. Nxd4 Bd7 {Solid.}) 4... Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 7. Qe2 Nd5 8. c4 Ba6 {Scotch main line. Pressure on c4.}) 3... Bc5 (3... Nf6 4. Ng5 {The Fried Liver try.} 4... d5 5. exd5 Na5 {NOT 5...Nxd5?, which allows 6.Nxf7! (you lost this way in 2026).} 6. Bb5+ c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. Be2 h6 9. Nf3 e4 10. Ne5 Bd6 {A pawn down, but big development and initiative.}) 4. c3 (4. b4 {Evans Gambit. Decline it.} 4... Bb6 5. a4 a6 6. Nc3 Nf6 {Safe. Black develops and castles.}) 4... Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 a6 8. Bb3 Ba7 9. h3 h6 {The quiet Italian. Mirror White and keep the tension.} *\n"},{"name":"Sicilian Najdorf (Black)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Sicilian Najdorf (Black)\"]\n\n1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 (2. c3 {Alapin.} 2... Nf6 3. e5 Nd5 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. cxd4 d6 7. Bc4 Nb6 8. Bb5 dxe5 9. Nxe5 Bd7 {Trade the e5 knight. The isolated d4 pawn is White's problem.}) (2. Nc3 {Closed / Grand Prix.} 2... d6 3. f4 (3. g3 Nc6 4. Bg2 g6 5. d3 Bg7 6. Be3 e5 {The Botvinnik set-up. Play ...Nge7, ...O-O and ...f5.}) 3... Nc6 4. Nf3 g6 5. Bb5 Bd7 6. O-O Bg7 7. d3 a6 8. Bxc6 Bxc6 {The bishop pair. ...Nf6 and ...O-O.}) (2. d4 cxd4 3. c3 {Smith-Morra Gambit.} 3... dxc3 4. Nxc3 Nc6 5. Nf3 d6 6. Bc4 e6 7. O-O Nf6 8. Qe2 Be7 9. Rd1 e5 {Take the pawn, close the d-file, castle. White's attack runs out.}) 2... d6 3. d4 (3. Bb5+ Bd7 4. Bxd7+ Qxd7 5. c4 Nc6 6. Nc3 g6 7. d4 Bg7 {Solid. ...Nf6 and ...O-O.}) (3. c3 Nf6 4. Be2 Nc6 5. d4 cxd4 6. cxd4 Nxe4 {A free pawn: d5 tricks don't work yet.}) 3... cxd4 4. Nxd4 (4. Qxd4 Nc6 5. Bb5 Bd7 6. Bxc6 Bxc6 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. Bg5 e6 9. O-O-O Be7 {The bishop pair and ...O-O.}) 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 {The Najdorf, the main weapon of Fischer and Kasparov. ...a6 stops Nb5 and Bb5 and prepares ...e5 or ...b5.} 6. Be3 (6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Be7 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O Nbd7 10. g4 b5 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 12. g5 Nd7 {Next ...Nc5 and ...b4. Opposite castling: a pure race, so every tempo counts.}) (6. Be2 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Be3 Be6 10. Qd2 Nbd7 11. a4 Rc8 {A healthy Najdorf: ...Qc7, ...Rfd8, then ...d5.}) (6. Bc4 {The Sozin, Fischer's own weapon as White.} 6... e6 7. Bb3 b5 8. O-O Be7 9. Qf3 Qc7 10. Qg3 O-O 11. Bh6 Ne8 {Black holds g7 and then plays ...Bb7 and ...Nd7.}) (6. h3 e5 7. Nde2 h5 8. g3 Be6 9. Bg2 Nbd7 {Stops g4. A normal Najdorf.}) (6. g3 e5 7. Nde2 Be7 8. Bg2 O-O 9. O-O b5 {Queenside space.}) (6. a4 e5 7. Nf3 Be7 8. Bc4 O-O 9. O-O Be6 {Trade the c4 bishop.}) (6. f3 e5 7. Nb3 Be6 8. Be3 {Transposes to the English Attack.}) 6... e5 7. Nb3 Be6 8. f3 Be7 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O Nbd7 11. g4 b5 12. g5 b4 13. Ne2 Ne8 {English Attack. A pure race: White pushes h4-h5 and g5, Black plays ...a5-a4 and ...Nc7 or ...d5. Know your plan and don't waste a tempo.} *\n"},{"name":"Sicilian Dragon (Black)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Sicilian Dragon (Black)\"]\n\n1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 {Anti-Sicilians (2.c3, 2.Nc3, 3.Bb5+, Smith-Morra): use the same answers as in the Najdorf file.} 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 {The Dragon. The g7 bishop breathes fire down the long diagonal.} 6. Be3 (6. Be2 {Classical.} 6... Bg7 7. O-O O-O 8. Be3 Nc6 9. Nb3 Be6 10. f4 Qc8 {Pressure on the c-file, then ...Rd8 and ...d5.}) (6. f4 {Levenfish.} 6... Nbd7 7. Nf3 Bg7 8. Bd3 O-O 9. O-O Nc5 {Hit the d3 bishop and play ...b5 or ...Bg4.}) (6. g3 Nc6 7. Nde2 Bg7 8. Bg2 O-O 9. O-O Rb8 {Then ...b5-b4.}) (6. Bc4 Bg7 7. O-O O-O 8. h3 Nc6 9. Be3 Bd7 {Normal development. ...Rc8 and ...Ne5.}) (6. Bg5 Bg7 7. Qd2 Nc6 8. Nb3 O-O 9. Be2 a5 {Queenside expansion.}) 6... Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 (9. O-O-O d5 {The thematic central break.} 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Bd4 Bxd4 13. Qxd4 Qb6 {Active play on the b-file.}) (9. g4 Be6 10. O-O-O Nxd4 11. Bxd4 Qa5 12. a3 Rfc8 {Counterattack on the queenside.}) 9... Bd7 10. O-O-O Rc8 11. Bb3 Ne5 12. Kb1 Nc4 13. Bxc4 Rxc4 14. g4 b5 {The Yugoslav Attack. A race: White goes h4-h5 and Bh6, Black throws everything at the white king with ...b4, ...Qa5 and exchange sacrifices on c3.} *\n"},{"name":"King's Indian Defence (Black)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: King's Indian Defence (Black)\"]\n\n1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 (2. Nf3 g6 3. Bf4 Bg7 4. e3 d6 5. h3 O-O 6. Be2 c5 7. c3 b6 8. O-O Bb7 9. Nbd2 Nbd7 {Against the London: solid, then ...a6 and ...b5, or ...e5.}) (2. Bg5 {Trompowsky.} 2... Ne4 3. Bf4 d5 4. e3 c5 5. Bd3 Nf6 6. c3 Nc6 7. Nd2 {Equal. Develop and castle.}) 2... g6 3. Nc3 (3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nc3 e5 8. e4 c6 9. h3 Qb6 {Fianchetto Variation, as in Botvinnik-Tal 1960 (Guess the move).}) 3... Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 (5. f3 {Sämisch.} 5... O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 (7. Nge2 c6 8. Qd2 Nbd7 9. O-O-O a6 10. Kb1 b5 {Counterattack on the queenside.}) 7... Nh5 8. Qd2 f5 9. O-O-O Nd7 10. Bd3 Nc5 11. Bc2 a6 {Play ...b5 and ...f4.}) (5. f4 {Four Pawns.} 5... O-O 6. Nf3 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Be2 exd5 9. cxd5 Re8 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Ng4 12. Bg5 Qb6 13. O-O Nxe5 {Black takes the centre pawn. Sharp but sound.}) (5. Be2 O-O 6. Bg5 {Averbakh.} 6... c5 7. d5 e6 8. Qd2 exd5 9. exd5 Re8 10. Nf3 Bg4 11. O-O Nbd7 {Benoni-type play.}) 5... O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O (7. d5 {Petrosian.} 7... a5 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 Na6 10. Nd2 Qe8 11. O-O Nh7 {Then ...f5.}) (7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Bg5 Re8 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. cxd5 c6 12. Bc4 cxd5 13. Bxd5 Nd7 {Equal. No danger for Black.}) (7. Be3 exd4 8. Nxd4 Re8 9. f3 c6 10. Qd2 d5 {The central break.}) 7... Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 (9. b4 {Bayonet.} 9... Nh5 10. g3 f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. f3 {Then ...f4 or ...c6.}) (9. Nd2 a5 10. a3 Nd7 11. Rb1 f5 12. b4 Kh8 {Kingside play comes first.}) 9... Nd7 10. Be3 f5 11. f3 f4 12. Bf2 g5 {The Mar del Plata, the King's Indian's most famous battle. White attacks on the queenside with c5. Black storms the kingside: ...Ng6, ...Rf6-h6, ...g4. Fischer, Tal and Kasparov all won brilliant games here.} *\n"},{"name":"Nimzo-Indian and Queen's Indian (Black)","side":"b","pgn":"[Event \"Repertoire: Nimzo-Indian and Queen's Indian (Black)\"]\n\n1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 (3. Nf3 b6 {Queen's Indian.} 4. g3 (4. a3 Bb7 5. Nc3 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Qc2 Nd7 8. Nxd5 exd5 9. g3 c5 {Active pieces.}) (4. e3 Bb7 5. Bd3 d5 6. O-O Bd6 7. b3 O-O 8. Bb2 Nbd7 {Solid.}) (4. Nc3 Bb4 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 Bb7 7. e3 g5 8. Bg3 Ne4 9. Qc2 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 d6 {Control of e4.}) 4... Ba6 5. b3 Bb4+ 6. Bd2 Be7 7. Bg2 c6 8. Bc3 d5 9. Ne5 Nfd7 10. Nxd7 Nxd7 11. Nd2 O-O 12. O-O {The classical main line. Black is solid and will play ...Rc8 and ...c5 or ...b5.}) (3. g3 {Catalan.} 3... d5 4. Bg2 Be7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. O-O dxc4 7. Qc2 a6 8. Qxc4 b5 9. Qc2 Bb7 10. Bd2 Be4 11. Qc1 {Black has neutralised the g2 bishop.}) 3... Bb4 {The Nimzo-Indian: pin the knight, fight for e4, and be ready to give the bishop for the knight to double White's pawns.} 4. e3 (4. Qc2 {Classical.} 4... O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 d5 (6... b6 7. Bg5 Bb7 8. f3 h6 9. Bh4 d5 {Pressure on the long diagonal.}) 7. Nf3 dxc4 8. Qxc4 b6 9. Bf4 Ba6 10. Qc2 Nbd7 {Then ...c5 and ...Rc8. Quick development makes up for White's two bishops (engine: equal).}) (4. a3 {Sämisch.} 4... Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 c5 6. e3 Nc6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Ne2 b6 9. e4 Ne8 {Capablanca's idea: blockade, then ...Ba6 and ...Na5 against the c4 pawn.}) (4. f3 d5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. dxc5 Qa5 9. e4 Ne7 10. Be3 O-O {Hit the weak c-pawns.}) (4. Bg5 h6 5. Bh4 c5 6. d5 d6 7. e3 exd5 8. cxd5 Nbd7 {Benoni-style play.}) (4. Nf3 O-O 5. Bg5 c5 6. e3 cxd4 7. exd4 h6 8. Bh4 d5 {Against the isolated pawn.}) (4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d5 6. Nf3 dxc4 7. O-O Nc6 {Hold the extra pawn for a while.}) 4... O-O 5. Bd3 (5. Ne2 d5 6. a3 Be7 7. cxd5 exd5 8. g3 c6 {Solid.}) 5... d5 6. Nf3 c5 7. O-O Nc6 8. a3 Bxc3 9. bxc3 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Qc7 {The Rubinstein main line. Black plays ...e5 to fight the centre, and the doubled c-pawns are a long-term target.} *\n"}];
